Here's the complete mettail-gillespie crate. Let me walk through the architecture and how it connects to the existing MeTTaIL prototype:

Architecture Overview
The extension has 7 modules organized in layers:

Core Types (bottom layer):

rate_value.rs — The RateValue enum: either Real(f64) where r ∈ [0,1], or Complex(Complex64) where |z|² ∈ [0,1]. This single type distinction drives the entire classical/quantum split.
spatial_behavior.rs — SpatialBehavior terms that serve as keys in rate maps. These refine the type on the LHS of a rule: Local(channel), Interaction{in, out}, Parallel, Sequential, Replicated, Guard, and Custom for language-defined behaviors. In the rho-calculus, the Interaction variant maps directly to the COMM rule's channel matching.
rate_map.rs — RateMap: the map SpatialBehavior → RateValue. Includes validation (no mixed modes, total probability ≤ 1), normalization, classical selection (cumulative distribution), composition (for sequential rules), and merge (for parallel composition).
Rule Layer:

augmented_rule.rs — AugmentedRewriteRule extends the existing rewrite rule with: (LHS_term, LHS_rate_map) ~> (RHS_term, RHS_rate_map). The AugmentedRewriteSystem collects rules and computes total propensities.
Simulation Layer:

gillespie.rs — Classical Gillespie SSA (stochastic π-machine style): exponential waiting times, cumulative distribution selection, three-level sampling (rule → spatial behavior → fire).
quantum.rs — Quantum CTMC simulation: QuantumState as superpositions with interference, GeneratorMatrix construction, amplitude evolution, and measurement/collapse. Destructive interference is properly handled — two paths to the same term with opposite amplitudes cancel.
simulator.rs — Unified Simulator that auto-detects mode from rate value types and dispatches accordingly.
Integration Layer:

language_ext.rs — Proposed where/producing syntax for the language! macro, a RuleBuilder API, and codegen reference showing what the proc macro should emit. Unannotated rules default to {null ↦ 1.0} for backward compatibility.
Key Design Decisions
The where/producing syntax on rewrite rules keeps the existing syntax valid (rules without annotations just work with rate

